"""
Checks for the object's state and its constructor.

A module used to serve a unit struct: the dispatch named the type as a value,
so there was nowhere to put configuration and nothing to carry it in. Now
`#[dagger::object]` on the `struct` declares the fields, `serve` selects
`currentFunctionCall.parent` and decodes it into the receiver, and a
`#[dagger::constructor]` fills it in once for the whole call.

Three things that only a live engine can show, and that `sdk/tests/state.rs`
cannot: that a constructor's arguments really do become the module's own flags,
that a declared field is answered by the engine without the module running at
all, and that the document a function returns comes back as the next call's
parent.
"""
type StateChecks {
  let harness: TestHarness!

  """
  The constructor should supply its defaults when the caller passes nothing.

  `shout` takes no arguments and reads two fields, so the value it returns can
  only have come through the constructor and the parent document.
  """
  pub constructorDefaultsReachTheFunction(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    harness.modTarget(ws).assertJsonString(
      ["shout"],
      "hello",
      "a function with no arguments should see the constructor's defaults",
    )
  }

  """
  A constructor argument should be spelled before the function, and reach it.

  This is the whole point of a constructor: `--greeting` and `--repeat` are the
  module's own flags rather than every function's, so they are written where
  the module is named.
  """
  pub constructorArgumentsConfigureTheModule(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let target = harness.modTarget(ws)

    target.assertJsonString(
      ["--greeting", "hi", "shout"],
      "hi",
      "a constructor argument should reach a function that never declared it",
    )
    target.assertJsonString(
      ["--greeting", "hi", "--repeat", "3", "shout"],
      "hihihi",
      "two constructor arguments should reach one function",
    )
  }

  """
  A declared field should be readable directly.

  `withField` is what makes this work, and it is not a function: the engine
  answers out of the state the module handed back, so `greeting` is a selection
  rather than a call. A field that was registered but never encoded — or encoded
  under a name the typedef does not have — fails here and nowhere else.
  """
  pub fieldsAreReadableWithoutAFunction(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    harness.modTarget(ws).assertJsonString(
      ["--greeting", "reachable", "greeting"],
      "reachable",
      "a pub field should be readable as a field of the module",
    )
  }

  """
  A function returning the object should hand back state the next call reads.

  The return goes out through `encode_state` rather than `encode_object`: there
  is no id for a module's own object, so what crosses is the document its fields
  encode to. `with-greeting shout` is that document making the round trip — out
  of one call, into the next one's `parent`.
  """
  pub returningTheObjectCarriesItsState(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let target = harness.modTarget(ws)

    target.assertJsonString(
      ["with-greeting", "--greeting", "chained", "shout"],
      "chained",
      "a returned object should carry the field the function set",
    )
    target.assertJsonString(
      ["with-greeting", "--greeting", "chained", "greeting"],
      "chained",
      "a returned object should be readable through its fields too",
    )
  }

  """
  Chained calls should each start from the last one's state.

  `louder` never mentions the greeting and `with-greeting` never mentions the
  count, so a value that survives both has been through the parent document
  twice. A dispatch that rebuilt the object from an empty state instead would
  answer the second call with the constructor's defaults, and the assertion
  below is what tells the two apart.
  """
  pub stateSurvivesAChainOfCalls(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    harness.modTarget(ws).assertJsonString(
      ["--greeting", "ha", "with-greeting", "--greeting", "he", "louder", "shout"],
      "hehe",
      "each call in a chain should start from the state the last one returned",
    )
  }

  """
  A field's deprecation and its doc comment should reach the schema.

  Neither has a behavioural shadow, so the schema the module serves is the only
  place to see whether `withField` was given them. `includeDeprecated: true` is
  as load-bearing here as it is for a deprecated function: GraphQL hides a
  deprecated field from introspection by default, so leaving it off looks
  exactly like a macro that never declared it.

  The doc half is asserted alongside because a field takes its description from
  its `///` comment rather than from a `#[dagger(doc = ...)]`, unlike every
  argument in this fixture — a field is state rather than an input, and Rust
  does have doc comments on fields.
  """
  pub fieldMetadataIsDeclared(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let target = harness.target(ws)
    let queried = target.query(
      "{__type(name:\"FeatureMatrix\"){fields(includeDeprecated:true){name description deprecationReason}}}",
    )

    queried.assertStdoutContains(
      "use greeting instead",
      "a #[dagger(deprecated = ...)] field should reach the schema as its deprecationReason",
    )
    queried.assertStdoutContains(
      "The greeting the state functions echo back.",
      "a field's /// comment should become its description",
    )
  }

  """
  The constructor should be registered as the module's own arguments.

  It is not one of the object's functions — the engine knows it by having no
  name at all — so it surfaces on the *root* field that builds the module rather
  than beside `shout` and `echo`. Asking the schema is what tells a constructor
  that registered from one the macro emitted as an ordinary function called
  `new`, which would answer `constructorArgumentsConfigureTheModule` above with
  a flag parse error rather than a wrong value.
  """
  pub theConstructorIsRegisteredOnTheRootField(ws: Workspace!): Void @check {
    let target = harness.target(ws)
    let queried = target.query("{__schema{queryType{fields{name args{name}}}}}")

    queried.assertStdoutContains(
      "greeting",
      "the constructor's arguments should be the module's root field's arguments",
    )
  }
}
